1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A Python coroutine needs a network address. It awaits a lookup, and the Pythago process can keep doing other work.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The event loop schedules tasks. Thread state lets a thread enter Python. A trampoline carries calls into Go.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:25,000
All three happen inside one process. Follow the lookup in order, from a suspended coroutine to a result that lets it resume.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:36,000
An asyncio event loop runs callbacks and steps tasks on its loop thread. Its ready queue holds work that can run now. A timer holds work until its deadline.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Task A starts a lookup. Its future is still pending, so await suspends the task. The event loop can now give task B a turn.

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:56,000
When the lookup finishes, setting the future result schedules the waiting task. The loop takes it from the ready queue and continues after the await.

7
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:07,000
This is cooperative scheduling. A long synchronous callback occupies the loop thread. The loop can only run the next callback when the current one returns or the task yields.

8
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Pythago provides two backends through goaio. Both preserve Python’s task and timer scheduling, but they hand different parts of the I/O work to Go.

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:29,000
In GoEventLoop, goroutines do operations such as dialing sockets and resolving DNS. They put completed results in a batch and wake the Python loop.

10
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:40,000
GoSelectorEventLoop goes through the standard selector loop. Go watches descriptor readiness. Once a descriptor is ready, Python makes the socket call.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:51,000
A timer can bound how long the loop waits. A thread-safe callback can wake it even when the I/O batch is empty. The loop then checks its ready queue.

12
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:01,000
A goroutine can normally move between operating system threads. CPython stores an attached thread state locally on the operating system thread that is using it.

13
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:10,000
An outer py.Do takes a slot, pins the goroutine to its current OS thread, and attaches that thread’s Python state.

14
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
On the pthread path, eligible Python-created threads use a Go goroutine locked to an operating system thread for their lifetime. Python keeps its thread identity.

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:29,000
GIL builds also need the lock. Free-threaded builds still attach state. Config.Slots separately limits how many outer Go calls can enter.

16
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:38,000
If a poll already has work, it returns straight away. If it has to wait, Pythago uses py.Release around the wait.

17
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Release detaches the thread state and returns any admission slot it held. Detaching also gives up the GIL on builds that use it.

18
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:56,000
While the loop waits for a wakeup or deadline, other goroutines can enter Python. The blocking section needs an attached state to access Python objects.

19
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Before going back to Python, Release reacquires any slot it returned and restores the thread state. Then the loop can dispatch the results on its own thread.

20
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:15,000
A trampoline is a short adapter at a call boundary. Go can call a CPython symbol using the C calling convention. Now follow a call in the other direction.

21
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:25,000
With py.NewFunc, a Go function becomes a Python builtin. These builtins share a C trampoline. Each carries the index of its Go function.

22
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:35,000
The trampoline packs the function index, positional arguments, and keywords into a call frame. It enters Go through crosscall2, or a Windows callback.

23
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Go dispatches to the registered function on the calling Python thread, with its state attached. The call is synchronous, even from a coroutine.

24
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Go returns a Python value or raises an exception. Borrowed arguments expire when the call returns unless explicitly retained.

25
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,000
A callback can start I/O and return immediately. If the work finishes later, the event loop handles that completion separately.

26
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:11,000
The coroutine calls getaddrinfo. The loop creates a future and registers a handler under a token. It calls Go to resolve the address, then awaits the future.

27
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:21,000
The Go call starts a lookup on a goroutine. If the loop has no ready work, poll can wait with the Python thread state detached.

28
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Go posts the token and result to the completion queue. The wakeup brings the loop back. It reattaches its state, reads the batch, and finds the handler for that token.

29
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:40,000
The handler completes the future with a result or exception. The waiting task goes back in the ready queue. Python resumes it after await.

30
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Start in getaddrinfo. The loop creates a future, registers a completion handler, and passes the handler token to the native resolve function.

31
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Await suspends the coroutine if the future is pending. The handler eventually removes its own registration and sets the result or exception on that same future.

32
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The token is a key in the current loop’s handler table. Each operation gets a new key, so several requests can be outstanding on the same loop.

33
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:20,000
GoEventLoop inherits Python’s base event loop. Its proactor adapter implements select by calling native poll with the timeout chosen by the loop.

34
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Each returned item names a token and event kind. Process events looks up the matching handler and calls it if it is still registered.

35
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:42,000
The selector backend looks different. It returns ready file descriptors and an event mask. The standard selector loop can then perform the socket operation itself.

36
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:52,000
In Go, resolve reads the arguments before it starts a goroutine. The callback returns immediately, while the worker calls the Go network resolver.

37
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:02,000
The worker posts a success or error with the original token. The queue holds Go data, which the loop later converts for Python.

38
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Posting takes the batch mutex. A full batch makes producers wait. When the batch changes from empty to nonempty, the producer sends a wakeup.

39
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:22,000
The wake channel has room for one signal. Kick sends without blocking. If a signal is already pending, it leaves that signal in place.

40
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Poll first tries to take queued work. If it found anything or the timeout is zero, it can return without waiting.

41
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Otherwise, the loop releases its Python state and waits for a wakeup or a timer. It drains the batch again after the wait, even if the wakeup carried no work.

42
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:54,000
An outer py.Do reserves an admission slot before locking the goroutine to its current operating system thread. The deferred cleanup reverses that setup.

43
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:04,000
If the same thread has already entered Python, a nested Do calls enter directly. It does not wait for another slot or repin the goroutine.

44
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:14,000
The Go enter helper asks C to attach. The C result distinguishes an attachment it just made from one it can reuse.

45
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:24,000
The C glue looks for a cached state on this thread. It creates one if needed, then restores it before Python code runs.

46
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:35,000
Release asks C to detach and remembers the reported slot. It returns that slot before calling the blocking function. Python access in that section needs a nested Do.

47
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:46,000
Deferred cleanup reacquires the slot if there was one, then restores the saved thread state. The original thread can continue using Python.

48
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:55,000
There can be several operating system threads in one process. Each row shows who starts the work and which thread owns the Python state.

49
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:04,000
A Go caller entering through py.Do is pinned to its current operating system thread. Pythago attaches a Python state on that thread.

50
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:13,000
On the pthread wrapper path, a locked goroutine runs Python’s native start routine. A spare can already be waiting for that assignment.

51
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Before calling the start routine, the wrapper drops any Python state cached by py.Do on that thread. CPython can then bind its own state.

52
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:32,000
On Windows, CPython creates its own native thread. The callback can still enter Go on that same thread while its Python state is attached.

53
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:42,000
A Go I/O worker can run without attaching Python state. The loop thread detaches its own state while it waits for work.

54
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:52,000
A Go goroutine has its own growable stack. On this call path, native code runs on the operating system thread’s system stack.

55
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:02,000
On Linux and macOS, the caller prepares a C call frame. The runtime switches to the system stack before entering the assembly thunk.

56
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:13,000
The thunk places arguments where the platform calling convention expects them. On amd64, the last two of its eight integer slots go on the stack.

57
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:24,000
When CPython calls the C trampoline, crosscall2 enters the Go callback. The native call is still pending on the same operating system thread.

58
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:34,000
The callback can grow and move the goroutine stack. The thunk compares the old and new stack tops before storing the C return value.

59
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:46,000
Windows takes a separate route through SyscallN. The runtime keeps that outbound call frame with the operating system thread, outside the movable goroutine stack.

60
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:56,000
NewFunc adds the Go function to a registry under a lock. Its index is the length of the registry before the append.

61
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:07,000
The native method definition points at the shared fast-call entry. The new Python builtin carries the function index as its self object.

62
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:18,000
When Python calls the builtin, the C trampoline writes the argument pointers, counts, keyword names, and the index into a call frame.

63
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:29,000
C decodes a small prefix of arguments and keyword names. Go can use these cached representations for common values while it still has the original objects.

64
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:40,000
The trampoline tracks callback depth while it calls the Go dispatcher. Windows uses a native callback pointer. Other targets here enter through crosscall2.

65
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:50,000
On the way back, a borrowed result needs a new Python reference. The trampoline increments its reference count before returning it to the interpreter.

66
00:10:50,000 --> 00:11:00,000
The Go entry reads the same call frame that C allocated. It writes the result and ownership flag into that frame for C to read.

67
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:10,000
The dispatcher uses the index to read the Go function from the registry. It drops the read lock before invoking the function.

68
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:20,000
The argument pointers are borrowed. Their wrappers can use the values during the call. A Go function must retain a reference to keep an object longer.

69
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:30,000
The positional slice and keyword map point to those wrappers. Keyword names map to slots after the positional arguments. The final call uses both collections.

70
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:40,000
An ordinary Go error becomes a Python exception. The deferred recover path also converts a Go panic and leaves a null result for Python.

71
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:52,000
The return path distinguishes an owned object from a borrowed object. An owned reference transfers to Python. A borrowed reference is marked so C can take a new reference.

72
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Poll calls wait and gets the batch after the thread state is attached again. This is where Go can safely turn event data into Python values.

73
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:11,000
The poll response is a list of tuples. Each tuple preserves the original token, the event kind, and the data the Python handler expects.

74
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:20,000
The lookup handler completes the future. Its waiter returns to the ready queue, and the coroutine picks up where it left off.

75
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:29,000
The loop decides what resumes. The operating system thread carries Python’s attached state. The trampoline adapts the synchronous call into Go.

76
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:40,000
When the next coroutine awaits a network result, follow the same path. Go finishes the operation, wakes the loop, and Python resumes the waiting task.
